  "articleBody": "Adding decorative molding transforms plain kitchen cabinets, but small installation errors quickly become obvious eyesores once the tools are put away.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n- Measuring only once: Wall runs and cabinet faces shift slightly from corner to corner. Always double-check measurements at both top and bottom edges before making your first cut.\n- Guessing the spring angle: Cabinet crown molding commonly rests at either 38 or 45 degrees. Cutting with the wrong angle setting produces corners that will never sit flush.\n- Skipping the dry-fit phase: Nailing trim without testing the joint fit leads to forced angles and split wood. Test-fitting scrap pieces confirms your saw adjustments before cutting final trim.\n- Fastening into drywall alone: Molding must anchor into solid cabinet rails or installed wood backer strips so it does not sag or pull away when the house settles.\n- Using caulk as a fix for wide gaps: Caulk handles tiny hairline joints, but using it to bridge major discrepancies looks messy and shrinks over time. Substantial ceiling dips require scribing or trim fillers instead.\n\nWhen ceiling lines are visibly out of level or custom finishes demand flawless miters, expert carpentry saves valuable materials. HH Remodeling LLC provides skilled finish carpentry throughout the Denver metro area. Call Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to plan your kitchen trim update.",
